  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThis Weekend...\nSurface high pressure remains centered over the lower Great \nLakes this afternoon, while aloft, an expansive ridge stretches \nfrom the Southeast CONUS to the Canadian Rockies. Meanwhile, a \ndeep upper low is positioned off the Pacific Northwest coast. \nWater vapor imagery shows a series of shortwave troughs buckling\nthe upper ridge as they lift towards and across the mid-\nMississippi Valley. Isolated to widely scattered showers will \npersist across central Illinois ahead of the first wave this \nafternoon and evening, lifting into northern Illinois tonight. A\nsecond wave will bring another round of showers primarily south\nof I-72 late tonight.\n\nWeak low-level lapse rates and dry air below 700 mb may yield \nmore virga than measurable rainfall today and tonight. HREF mean\n24-hour QPF averages under 0.10 inches through Sunday morning, \nthough LPMM guidance indicates isolated swaths up to 0.25 inches\nare possible within heavier showers. Weak instability (<100 \nJ/kg) and low equilibrium levels preclude thunder mentions \nthrough tonight. Lingering showers Sunday morning will remain \nlight, tapering off from the west by Sunday afternoon as the \ntrailing shortwave clears and upper ridging rebuilds. Despite \ncloud cover and light rain, temperatures remain above normal \nwith highs in the 60s, reaching the lower 70s south of I-70.\n\nEarly Next Week...\nA pair of potent mid-level shortwaves/closed H5 lows will dig \ninto the western CONUS early next week--one tracking across the\nIntermountain West and another across the Canadian Rockies. \nModel guidance continues to exhibit spread regarding the \ninteraction and phasing of these waves, leading to variance in \nsurface patterns for the mid-to-late week period. Nevertheless, \nbroad mid-level height falls over the Rockies will induce \nsurface cyclogenesis over eastern Colorado early in the week.\n\nThe surface low is forecast to deepen as it lifts into the \nUpper Midwest Tuesday, though model disparity increases \nregarding system track and intensity. Stronger solutions favor \nbreezy to windy conditions across central Illinois on Tuesday; \nNBM probabilities for wind gusts exceeding 40 mph range from \n40-60% across a good portion of the area. Warm advection ahead \nof the system will push highs into the mid 70s Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nLate Next Week...\nA cold front associated with the elongated surface trough will \ncross central Illinois in the Wednesday to Thursday timeframe, \nwith timing trending slightly faster in recent model suites. \nTemperatures well into the 60s ahead of the boundary will fall \nback into the 50s post-frontal. The frontal passage will signal \nthe end of widespread precipitation, though lingering low-level \ncyclonic flow and cold air advection may maintain stratocumulus \ncover along with a few light sprinkles into late week.",
